1. catch returns the value thrown to it, or the block's value
   with a throw:    20
   without a throw: "block finished without a throw"
2. escaping nested loops
   visiting 1,1
   visiting 1,2
   visiting 1,3
   visiting 2,1
   visiting 2,2
   first cell whose product is 4: [2, 2]
3. a throw with no matching catch is an exception, and a StandardError
   UncaughtThrowError: "uncaught throw :nope"; tag :nope, value 1
   e.is_a?(ArgumentError) true
4. a matched throw is not seen by a rescue in between, but ensure runs
   ensure ran on the way out
   catch returned "thrown past the rescue"
5. catch with no tag makes one and hands it to the block
   catch { |tag| throw tag, 7 }   -> 7
   the tag is a plain Object
6. throw with no value throws nil
   catch(:t) { throw :t }         -> nil
7. tags match by identity, which is why symbols are used
   catch("s") { throw "s".dup, 1 } -> UncaughtThrowError: "uncaught throw \"s\""
   the same String object       -> 1
   :s.equal?(:s)                true
8. throw unwinds through method calls
   catch(:bottom) { descend(5) } -> "reached the bottom"
